\section{Pseudocount}{

A zero in any numerator or denominator feature makes its geometric mean
0, so zeros need replacing first. With \code{pseudocount = NULL} (the
default), no pseudocount is used when \code{counts} has no zeros; otherwise
half the smallest non-zero value of the \strong{whole table} is added to
every value, with a warning, exactly as \code{\link[=ilr]{ilr()}} does, so a balance
agrees with the matching \code{ilr()} coordinate. To avoid a pseudocount,
replace the zeros first with \code{\link[=replace_zeros]{replace_zeros()}}: its result has none.
With \code{pseudocount = 0}, samples with a zero in a selected feature get
\code{NA}, with one warning naming them.

The choice of pseudocount is not neutral: it can distort the results,
especially for sparse tables. See \code{\link[=aitchison]{aitchison()}} for references.
}

\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

